# Rust build output. spikes/runtime/target alone is ~2 GB (ort vendors onnxruntime).
target/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Corpus: raw IR captures
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ~20 MB per 3-second take and they contain the operator's face. The .json
# sidecars are kept deliberately -- they are tiny, carry no biometric data, and
# are the only record of what was in front of the camera for each capture.
#
# CI needs *some* corpus to replay the liveness assertions against. That is an
# open question (committed subset from a consenting contributor, or a release-asset
# tarball fetched by the harness) -- see corpus/README.md.
corpus/*.gray
!corpus/*.json
!corpus/README.md

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Models: fetched from upstream, not authored here
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ship as a separate faciolock-models package with vendored licences and a
# SHA256SUMS verified at daemon startup (wiki/04). Provenance and licence status
# for each file is tracked in models/README.md, which IS committed.
models/*.onnx
!models/README.md

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Editors, OS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
*.swp
*~
.idea/
.vscode/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NEVER commit
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Enrolled templates are biometric data. Even TPM-sealed they do not belong in a
# repository, and an unsealed one is a face embedding in plaintext.
*.tpl
/var/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Golden references derived from real faces
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# detect_golden.txt holds box and landmark coordinates measured from the corpus
# recordings, so it is face-derived and stays local. Regenerate it with
# tools/gen-goldens.py. The alignment golden beside it IS committed -- it is a
# synthetic checkerboard and contains no biometric data at all.
crates/faciolock-vision/testdata/detect_golden.txt

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Credentials — NEVER commit
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# An OpenSSH private key was committed and pushed on 2026-08-06 in commit "readme",
# as `yes` / `yes.pub` — an `ssh-keygen` run where "yes" was typed at the filename
# prompt, then swept up by `git add -A`. It was passphrase-protected, which bought
# time and nothing more; the key still had to be rotated, because a passphrase is an
# offline-brute-forceable last line and not a control.
#
# Patterns rather than those two filenames, because the next accident will have a
# different name.
id_rsa
id_dsa
id_ecdsa
id_ed25519
*.pem
*.key
*_rsa
*_ed25519
.ssh/
# Any file whose first line is an OpenSSH/PEM private-key header cannot be matched by
# a glob, so the real defence is `git add -p` or `git add <path>` over `git add -A`.
#
# And the two actual filenames, because none of the patterns above match a key called
# `yes`. That is the point: the pattern list only catches keys that were named like keys.
yes
yes.pub

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Agent scratch
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Session logs and task trackers are working notes, not project documentation --
# what they establish belongs in wiki/ and in the bug log, which are committed.
.claude/

# Python bytecode from the throwaway spikes.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
